#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# sort

#% text
# `sort(xs)` gives the elements of `xs` in canonical order.

#% text
# `sort(xs, by)` orders them so that `by(a, b)` is true for each element `a` placed before `b`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The canonical order puts numbers first, by value, then other terms in a fixed structural order; strings are ordered alphabetically: letter case is ignored first, then lower case comes before upper case (`"cat"`, `"Cat"`, `"catfish"`); letters outside the basic alphabet keep their character order.

#% item
# Exact and inexact numbers are compared by value.

#% item
# With a comparison `by`, typically `(a, b) => a > b` for decreasing order, the sort is stable.

#% item
# To order by a computed key, `sort_by` is simpler; for the positions of the sorted elements, `ordering`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
sort([3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6])
#% output
#= [1, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 9]

#% cell
sort(["pear", "apple", "fig"])
#% output
#= ["apple", "fig", "pear"]

#% cell
sort([3, 1, 2], (a, b) => a > b)
#% output
#= [3, 2, 1]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Words in alphabetical order:

#% cell
sort(["cat", "fish", "catfish", "Cat"])
#% output
#= ["cat", "Cat", "catfish", "fish"]

#% text
# Numbers of every kind, by value:

#% cell
sort([1/2, 0.4, sqrt(2), 1, -3])
#% output
#= [-3, 0.4, 1/2, 1, 2^(1/2)]

#% text
# Symbols and expressions in canonical order:

#% cell
sort([c, b, a, x^2, 2])
#% output
#= [2, a, b, c, x^2]

#% text
# Lists are compared element by element:

#% cell
sort([[2, 1], [1, 5], [1, 2]])
#% output
#= [[1, 2], [1, 5], [2, 1]]

#% text
# The values of an association, keeping their keys:

#% cell
sort(assoc(a -> 3, b -> 1, c -> 2))
#% output
#= assoc(b -> 1, c -> 2, a -> 3)

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The median by hand, from the sorted data:

#% cell
data = [7, 2, 9, 4, 5]
sort(data)[3]
#% output
#= 5

#% text
# Points ordered by distance from the origin:

#% cell
sort([[3, 4], [1, 1], [0, 2]], (p, q) => norm(p) < norm(q))
#% output
#= [[1, 1], [0, 2], [3, 4]]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# Sorting keeps every element, repeated ones included; `unique` removes repetitions:

#% cell
xs = [2, 1, 2, 3, 1]
[sort(xs), sort(unique(xs))]
#% output
#= [[1, 1, 2, 2, 3], [1, 2, 3]]

#% text
# Sorting a sorted list changes nothing:

#% cell
sort(sort(xs)) === sort(xs)
#% output
#= true

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `sort_by`, `ordering`, `reverse`, `max`, `min`, `unique`
